# 4. DATA MODEL

All tables bitemporal and append-only unless stated. `valid_from`/`valid_to` =
business time. `recorded_at` = system time. **No `UPDATE` on any table below
except `queue_projection` and `review_outcome` status fields.**

```sql
-- Read-only projection of CAL. Written only by Stage A. Never by anything else.
alert_fact (
  alert_id            PK
  alert_type          enum CANCEL|AMEND|LATE
  alert_ts, event_ts, booking_ts, execution_ts
  trade_urn, alt_trade_id, lifecycle_ref, package_id, allocation_group
  trader_id, desk, book, counterparty_id, product_key, asset_class
  notional, currency, direction, price_or_rate
  amended_fields      jsonb      -- AMEND only
  lateness_minutes                -- LATE only
  cancel_reason_code              -- CANCEL only
  free_text           jsonb       -- untrusted; see §9.4
  entitlement_class
  source_system, source_ruleset_version
  blocklist_hits      jsonb       -- which identifiers were suppressed
  ingest_ts, recorded_at
)
INDEX (trade_urn), (alt_trade_id), (trader_id, event_ts), (package_id)

-- Emitted by Stage B (already built). Consume; do not write.
episode (
  episode_id, episode_version   PK composite
  lineage_root_id               -- USE THIS for all history
  status              enum PROVISIONAL|STABLE|FROZEN
  supersedes          text[]
  continuation_of
  as_of_ts                       -- authoritative cutoff
  ruleset_version, blocklist_version
  structural_facts    jsonb
  entitlement_class
  recorded_at
)
INDEX (lineage_root_id), (status, as_of_ts)

episode_member (episode_id, episode_version, alert_id, ordinal)
episode_edge   (episode_id, episode_version, rule_id, tier, confidence, ...)
episode_related(episode_id, related_episode_id, suppression_reason)

-- Stage C
signal_snapshot (
  episode_id, episode_version, signal_set_version   PK composite
  as_of_ts
  values              jsonb   -- NAMED keys, never a positional array
  peer_level_used, peer_population_n
  missing             jsonb   -- which signals were unavailable and why
  recorded_at
)

-- Stage D
score_result (
  episode_id, episode_version, scoring_version      PK composite
  rule_score, rule_components jsonb
  if_score, if_percentile, if_model_version         -- null when flag off
  final_score, evidence_band
  degraded boolean, degraded_reason
  signal_set_version, signal_snapshot_ref
  scored_at, as_of_ts
)

-- Stage E. The ONLY mutable table. Rebuildable from score_result at any time.
queue_projection (
  episode_id          PK
  episode_version, lineage_root_id
  bucket, urgency_band, evidence_band
  deadline, deadline_source enum EXPLICIT|DERIVED|ABSENT, deadline_member_id
  sort_key            -- materialised composite, see §8.3
  override_rule_id, override_reason
  promoted boolean, promotion_held_reason
  review_status
  updated_at
)
INDEX (bucket, sort_key), (review_status)

-- Stage F
evidence_pack (episode_id, episode_version, pack_version, content jsonb,
                tool_trace jsonb, built_at)
narrative     (episode_id, episode_version, narrative_version,
                agent_model_version, prompt_version, text,
                source_pack_ref, validation_result jsonb, generated_at)
agent_trace   (trace_id PK, episode_id, agent_name, tool_calls jsonb,
                tokens_in, tokens_out, latency_ms, outcome, recorded_at)

-- Stage H
review_outcome (
  episode_id, episode_version
  disposition enum SIGNOFF|RFI|MOVE|NONE
  grouping_feedback enum ACCEPT|SHOULD_SPLIT|SHOULD_MERGE
  explanation_useful boolean
  priority_feedback enum TOO_HIGH|RIGHT|TOO_LOW
  supervisor_id, notes, decided_at
  displayed_narrative_ref, displayed_score_ref   -- what they actually saw
)
```

`displayed_narrative_ref` and `displayed_score_ref` are mandatory. In an audit
the question is not what the system would produce today — it is what the
supervisor was shown at the moment they decided.

---

# 5. STAGE A — INGEST AND NORMALISE

## 5.1 Responsibilities

1. Consume the CAL CDC feed. **Read-only. No write path to CAL may exist in the
   codebase.** Enforce with a lint rule or a repository-layer guard, not a
   convention.
2. Map to `alert_fact`.
3. Identifier hygiene.
4. Time normalisation.
5. Validate; route failures to DLQ.

## 5.2 Identifier hygiene

Before any identifier becomes a match key, test against a **versioned
blocklist**: empty string, `N/A`, `NA`, `UNKNOWN`, `NULL`, `NONE`, whitespace
only, all-zeros, all-nines, house/internal counterparty codes, `MISC`, `OTHER`,
shared suspense books. Record every hit in `blocklist_hits`.

This single control prevents more grouping-explosion incidents than every
threshold combined. Treat the blocklist as living configuration with a named
owner.

**Identifier trust registry**, keyed `(source_system, identifier_name)`, carrying
a trust level that can downgrade a strong identifier to corroborative evidence
for that source. Without it, one bad upstream feed silently poisons
high-confidence linking and you learn about it from a supervisor complaint
rather than a metric.

## 5.3 Time normalisation

- All timestamps to UTC, originating timezone retained in a sibling column
- **Business date from the firm calendar, not from the timestamp's date.** A
  Tokyo booking at 23:50 UTC and a London booking at 00:10 UTC may or may not be
  the same business day, and business date is a linking key.
- Reject timestamps outside a configured plausible range rather than storing them

## 5.4 Error taxonomy

| Class | Example | Handling |
|---|---|---|
| `SCHEMA_INVALID` | missing required field | DLQ, ops exception |
| `IDENTIFIER_DEGENERATE` | all identifiers blocklisted | ingest, flag, alert is linkable only by weak evidence |
| `TIMESTAMP_IMPLAUSIBLE` | event_ts in 2087 | DLQ, ops exception |
| `UNKNOWN_ALERT_TYPE` | not CANCEL/AMEND/LATE | DLQ, ops exception |
| `DUPLICATE` | same alert_id already present | idempotent no-op, counter increment |

Never default a missing identifier to a placeholder. That is how degenerate keys
are born.

## 5.5 Idempotency

Key: `(alert_id, source_ruleset_version)`. Replay commits nothing new.

---

# 6. STAGE B — EPISODE LINKING (contract only)

**Already built. Do not modify.** Consume the sealed payload.

```
episode_id              stable per identity
lineage_root_id         stable across merges — USE THIS for history
episode_version         monotonic
supersedes[]            predecessor episode_ids
continuation_of         prior episode_id if formed after seal or freeze
status                  PROVISIONAL | STABLE | FROZEN
as_of_ts                authoritative data cutoff — never wall clock
ruleset_version, blocklist_version

members[]               PRE-ORDERED by (event_ts, booking_ts, alert_id) ASC
                        full alert_fact projection per member

structural_facts        computed by linking — NEVER re-derive downstream
  type_signature        multiset, e.g. {CANCEL:1, LATE:1}
  size, span_seconds
  s_edge_count, m_edge_count, max_tier, min_edge_confidence
  relationship_density
  flags[]               OVERSIZED | CAP_APPLIED | HUB_DEMOTED |
                        BRIDGE_SPLIT | DEGENERATE_KEY_SEEN

related_episodes[]      cross-references from suppressed merges, with reasons
edges[]                 rule_ids, tiers, confidences — for linkage rationale
entitlement_class       most restrictive across members
```

**Properties you may rely on:**
- Any episode of size ≥ 3 contains at least one deterministic identifier match.
  Medium-evidence-only episodes cap at size 2.
- `PROVISIONAL` episodes may still change composition.
- `member_ordering_key` is `(event_ts, booking_ts, alert_id)` ascending. Signal
  store, UI and narrative must all use it so "first" and "last" agree.

**Rules you inherit:**
- `lineage_root_id`, never `episode_id`, for recurrence — counting `episode_id`s
  double-counts merged predecessors
- `as_of_ts` is the cutoff for every computed signal; wall clock leaks
  look-ahead and invalidates replay

---

# 7. STAGE C — SIGNAL STORE

Precomputed comparative aggregates. **Not a general feature platform.** Its only
job is to answer cheaply and exactly the questions that make an episode look
unusual.

## 7.1 Point-in-time correctness

Every aggregate honours the episode's `as_of_ts`:

```
WHERE recorded_at <= :as_of_ts
```

This is the single most common source of a system that validates beautifully and
fails in production. Enforce with a property test (§13.2), not discipline.

## 7.2 Signal catalogue

### Trader baselines — window `signal.trader_baseline_window_days`
```
notional_p50, notional_p95, notional_current_percentile
lateness_p50, lateness_p95, lateness_current_z     (window: lateness window)
alert_count_30d, alert_count_90d          by alert_type and total
amend_rate, cancel_rate, economic_amend_share
tenure_days
```

### Peer comparison
```
peer_key = (desk, asset_class, seniority_band)
if population < signal.peer_min_population:
    fall back desk → business_line → firm
ALWAYS record peer_level_used and peer_population_n
```

Recording the level is not optional. The narrative must state the actual
comparison population — "compared to your desk (n=47)" and "compared to the firm
(n=12,400)" are materially different claims, and a supervisor challenged in an
audit needs to know which they were shown.

### Episode signals
```
type_signature                    copied from structural_facts, NOT recomputed
signature_recurrence_30d / _90d   same trader, same signature, BY lineage_root_id
opening_notional, closing_notional  first/last member under member_ordering_key
net_notional_delta_pct, direction_flipped, net_materiality
single_counterparty, single_product
days_to_period_end, is_month_end, is_quarter_end
crosses_valuation_cutoff
```

### Per-type signals
```
CANCEL:  time_to_cancel_sec, was_rebooked, rebook_price_delta_bps,
         rebook_notional_delta_pct, post_valuation_cutoff,
         cancel_reason_rarity
AMEND:   amend_sequence_no, is_economic_amend (economic field set is CONFIG —
         it is a compliance-visible definition and it will change),
         notional_delta_pct, price_delta_bps, counterparty_changed,
         book_changed, direction_changed, time_to_amend_sec, favourability
LATE:    lateness_minutes, lateness_z_vs_trader_baseline (more informative than
         absolute), booked_outside_hours, spans_valuation_cutoff,
         lateness_pctl_desk
```

### Series signals
```
rolling z-score of daily alert counts per (trader_id, alert_type)
                                     and (desk, alert_type)
window: signal.series_zscore_window_days
insufficient history → null + insufficient_history marker, NEVER imputed
```

## 7.3 Missingness

A missing input produces an **explicit null plus a `missing` entry naming the
reason**, never a silent zero. Zero and unknown are different facts and every
downstream consumer — including the Analyst agent — must be able to tell them
apart.

## 7.4 Efficiency

- Cache per-alert signals by `(alert_id, signal_set_version)`. Episodes
  re-version frequently; their members' per-alert signals do not change.
- **Batch rolling-window aggregates.** One query per episode is the dominant
  cost and the thing that will fall over at volume. Compute trader and peer
  baselines on a schedule into a materialised table; look them up at episode
  time.
- Recompute from scratch only on `signal_set_version` change.
- Target: p95 ≤ 2s per episode version.

---

# 8. STAGE D — SCORE

## 8.1 Rule baseline — mandatory, always runs

Policy-defined severity from configured rules. Fully explainable by
construction. Config-driven weights over named components:

```
economic_amend_weight        × is_economic_amend
lateness_band_weight         × lateness_z bucket
cancel_after_cutoff_weight   × post_valuation_cutoff
recurrence_weight            × signature_recurrence_30d threshold
materiality_weight           × notional_current_percentile band
episode_complexity_weight    × size, span
```

Emit `rule_components` as a named breakdown, not just a total. This *is* the
explanation in rule-only mode.

## 8.2 Isolation Forest — optional, flag-gated

`scoring.isolation_forest.enabled`. When false, no ML code path executes, no
artifact loads, and the system has no ML dependency at all. Verify with a test
that boots the app with the flag off and asserts the ML module is never imported.

When enabled:

- **Training is offline, scheduled, governed. Inference never fits anything.**
  If any inference path calls `.fit()`, the design is wrong — it produces
  non-deterministic scores, unbounded latency, and an unauditable model.
- Hash-pinned artifact verified at load; **refuse to start on mismatch**
- `signal_set_version` on the artifact must match the incoming snapshot;
  mismatch is a **hard failure**, not a warning — scoring a v4 snapshot with a
  v3 model produces a number that looks fine and means nothing
- Pinned `random_state`; pinned thread count where tree ordering is
  thread-dependent
- Subsample via `max_samples` — documented-correct behaviour, not a shortcut
- Vectorised batch scoring, never row-by-row
- Persisted scaler applied at inference, **never refit**
- TreeSHAP for explanations — exact, not approximated
- Percentile against a **frozen reference window**, refreshed only on the
  governance cadence. A rolling window makes scores incomparable across time and
  silently breaks every trend report.

## 8.3 Combination

Configured rule, not a learned ensemble:

```
final_score = rule_baseline × scoring.rule_baseline.weight
            + if_percentile × scoring.isolation_forest.weight   (when enabled)
renormalise weights when IF is disabled or unavailable
```

## 8.4 Numerical hygiene

- Validate the signal snapshot at the model boundary: no NaN, no inf, all
  required signals present per schema, all within configured plausible ranges
- Clip to configured bounds and **record every clip** — a silently clipped input
  produces a silently wrong explanation
- Reject to a scoring DLQ on schema violation. Do not coerce.

## 8.5 Degradation ladder

```
IF unavailable / times out  → rule baseline alone, degraded=true,
                              degraded_reason recorded
Signal snapshot incomplete  → score from available signals, mark which were
                              missing, degraded=true
Scoring stage down entirely → queue renders with SLA-only ordering
```

**Non-negotiable:**
- A degraded score is never rendered as a normal score. Visible badge in queue
  row and detail view.
- A degraded episode ranks **no worse than median priority**. A scoring failure
  must never bury an episode.
- Degradation raises an ops alert and is a tracked KPI.
- Ingestion and linking never block on scoring failure.

---

# 9. STAGE E — PRIORITISATION

## 9.1 Bands

```
Urgency (time to deadline, business days):
  OVERDUE   < 0
  CRITICAL  <= priority.urgency.critical_business_days
  NEAR      <= priority.urgency.near_business_days
  STANDARD  greater
  UNDATED   no deadline resolved

Evidence (final_score percentile):
  HIGH      >= priority.evidence.high_percentile
  ELEVATED  >= priority.evidence.elevated_percentile
  NORMAL    below
```

## 9.2 Buckets

| Bucket | Condition |
|---|---|
| B0 | any override fires (§9.4) |
| B1 | urgency OVERDUE or CRITICAL |
| B2 | evidence HIGH, urgency NEAR or STANDARD |
| B3 | evidence ELEVATED |
| B4 | evidence NORMAL, urgency STANDARD |

B0–B2 are the prioritised set; B3–B4 are not.

## 9.3 Sort key — strict total order

```
1. bucket ordinal
2. urgency band ordinal
3. final_score                DESC
4. max member alert score     DESC
5. episode size               DESC
6. earliest member alert_ts   ASC    (anti-starvation)
7. episode_id                 ASC    (deterministic tiebreak)
```

**Step 7 is not cosmetic.** Without a total order, paginated queues duplicate and
skip rows between page loads. Supervisors experience that as the system losing
their work, and it is the fastest way to lose adoption.

Materialise the composite into `queue_projection.sort_key` so pagination is a
single indexed range scan, not a sort at read time.

**Anti-starvation:** unreviewed beyond `priority.starvation_escalate_days`
escalates one urgency band regardless of evidence.

## 9.4 Overrides

Policy, not statistics. Deterministic config, evaluated outside any model, not
suppressible by a low score:

- any member alert has an open RFI
- trader / book / counterparty on a watchlist or under investigation
- episode crosses a regulatory reporting deadline or period-end valuation cutoff
- notional above firm materiality `[UNKNOWN #2]`
- repeat pattern: same trader, same `type_signature`, ≥ N in trailing 30d,
  **by `lineage_root_id`**
- manual supervisor pin

All force B0. The firing rule is always displayed as the placement reason.

**Bound them.** Alarm when B0 exceeds `priority.b0_share_alarm_pct`. An override
firing on a third of the queue has silently disabled prioritisation entirely, and
nothing else in the system will tell you.

## 9.5 Deadlines

**Episode deadline = earliest member deadline, always.** Store and display
`deadline_member_id`.

Resolution order:
1. **Explicit** from the alert → `deadline_source = EXPLICIT`
2. **Derive**: `event_ts + policy SLA for alert_type` `[UNKNOWN #1]` →
   `DERIVED`
3. **Default**: `alert_ts + priority.default_deadline_business_days` →
   `ABSENT`, band `UNDATED`, sorted at the **top** of STANDARD, never the bottom
4. Emit a data-quality exception; track undated rate as a KPI

Never treat an absent deadline as infinite. Never let missing data bury an
episode. `ABSENT` is a defect to drive to zero, not a steady state.

## 9.6 Promotion

Score every version immediately — nothing invisible. But **hold promotion into
the ranked queue until `status = STABLE`**, unless urgency reaches
`queue.promote_provisional_when_urgency_at`.

Reason: scoring a `PROVISIONAL` episode means its score moves under the
supervisor as members join, and on merge the row vanishes and a different
`episode_id` replaces it. Record `promotion_held_reason` so the state is
explicable.

---

# 10. STAGE F — AGENT LAYER

The part most likely to be built wrong. Read this section fully before writing
any of it.

## 10.1 Hard architectural constraints

1. **No write tools. Anywhere. At all.** Not "restricted write tools." None.
   This is the structural mitigation for prompt injection (§10.6) and the reason
   the constraint is architectural rather than a preference.
2. **No agent produces a number.** Every statistic comes from a tool result.
3. **Tools return computed answers, never raw row sets.**
   `get_trader_baseline` returns p50, p95 and the current percentile. It does
   not return 500 trades for the model to eyeball. An LLM shown raw history will
   approximate a percentile badly and confidently.
4. **Every factual claim must trace to a tool result**, validated
   programmatically before display — not by instructing the model to be careful.
5. Pinned `agent.model_version` and `prompt_version`, `temperature = 0`.
6. Full tool-call trace persisted as the audit artifact.
7. **The queue renders and ranks correctly with the layer switched off.** Assert
   this in an integration test.

## 10.2 Tool surface — read-only, typed

```
get_episode(episode_id)
  → sealed payload: members, structural_facts, edges, related_episodes

get_trader_baseline(trader_id, metric, as_of_ts)
  → { p50, p95, current_value, current_percentile, window_days, n }

get_peer_comparison(episode_id, metric)
  → { peer_level_used, peer_population_n, peer_p50, peer_p95,
      current_percentile }

get_recurrence(lineage_root_id, type_signature, window_days)
  → { count, prior_episode_refs[] }

get_related_episodes(episode_id)
  → [{ related_episode_id, suppression_reason, shared_attributes }]

get_free_text(episode_id)
  → [{ source, field, content, UNTRUSTED: true }]     -- see §10.6

get_prior_outcomes(trader_id, window_days)
  → [{ disposition, decided_at, episode_ref }]

get_score_breakdown(episode_id, episode_version)
  → { rule_components{}, if_percentile?, shap_top_k?, degraded, degraded_reason }
```

Every tool:
- takes `as_of_ts` or derives it from the episode; never uses wall clock
- enforces the caller's entitlement, independently of the agent
- returns bounded output — configured max array lengths
- times out at `agent.tool_timeout_ms`
- is logged into `agent_trace.tool_calls` with arguments and results

## 10.3 The four agents

### Case builder
Input: `episode_id`. Calls retrieval tools to assemble a verified
`evidence_pack`. Output is **structured data, not prose**. Deterministic tool
sequence where possible — this agent is closer to an orchestrator than a
reasoner, and if your framework allows it as plain code with no LLM call, prefer
that.

### Analyst
Input: `evidence_pack`. Output: business narrative plus a "why this is unusual"
write-up. Reads free text — cancel reason comments, trader annotations, prior
case notes — which nothing in the spine can interpret. **This is the strongest
justification for the entire layer.**

**Explains the score. Never produces or adjusts it.** If the narrative implies a
different priority than the queue shows, that is a bug in the narrative.

### Review assistant
Interactive during supervisor review. "Has this trader done this before?" → tool
call → grounded answer with trace attached. Multi-turn, but every turn
re-validated.

### Drafting
RFI and note drafts. The supervisor edits and sends. **The agent never sends.**

## 10.4 Output validation — mandatory, programmatic

Before any agent output reaches a screen:

```
1. Extract every numeric literal, date, identifier and named entity from output
2. Assert each appears in the tool trace for that episode
3. On any unsourced value:
     agent.validation.reject_on_unsourced_number = true → reject, retry once,
       then fall back to templated output
     false → flag inline in the UI
4. Persist validation_result on the narrative row regardless of outcome
```

A narrative that cannot be reconstructed from its stored pack and trace is not
auditable and must not ship.

## 10.5 Determinism and cost

- `temperature = 0`, pinned model version. Narratives remain non-reproducible in
  principle; **this is accepted because they are stored as artifacts of record,
  not regenerated.** Never claim a narrative can be regenerated.
- Budget: `agent.max_tool_calls_per_episode`, `agent.total_budget_ms`. Exceeding
  either aborts to templated output.
- Cache narratives by `(episode_id, episode_version, prompt_version,
  agent_model_version)`. Do not regenerate on every queue read.
- Generate lazily on episode open, not eagerly for the whole queue, unless
  measured demand justifies it.

## 10.6 Prompt injection — treat free text as hostile

Trader comments and counterparty-supplied fields are **untrusted input reaching
an instruction-following system**.

```
Controls, in order of strength:
1. NO WRITE TOOLS ANYWHERE — structural, cannot be defeated by clever text
2. Hard delimiting with explicit untrusted marking in the prompt
3. Output validation (§10.4) — an injected claim has no supporting tool result
4. Entitlement enforced in the tool layer, not the prompt
5. Trace review sampling
```

Do not rely on prompt instructions alone. An instruction telling the model to
ignore instructions in the data is the weakest control on this list.

## 10.7 Degradation

Agent layer unavailable, over budget, or failing validation → fall back to:
- templated linkage rationale (from `edges[]`)
- `rule_components` breakdown as the score explanation
- no narrative, with a visible "narrative unavailable" state

The supervisor's workflow continues unimpaired. Never block review on an agent.

---

# 11. STAGE G — SUPERVISOR UI

## 11.1 Four visual registers, consistently applied

| Register | Content | Treatment |
|---|---|---|
| SCP fact | original alert attributes | primary, unmarked |
| Inferred relationship | grouping, edges, confidence | distinct border/background, confidence shown |
| Model output | score, band, SHAP | distinct, with degraded badge when applicable |
| Generated narrative | Analyst output | visibly labelled as generated |

A supervisor must be able to tell at a glance which of the four they are reading.
This is a regulatory expectation, not a design preference.

## 11.2 Queue row

Episode ID, deadline with `deadline_source` indicator, score with band, highest
member alert score, alert count, `type_signature`, review status, badges:
`PROVISIONAL`, `DEGRADED`, `OVERSIZED`, `OVERRIDE`.

## 11.3 Detail view

Linked alerts in `member_ordering_key` order, episode timeline, trade context,
**linkage rationale with grouping certainty (`max_tier`,
`min_edge_confidence`)**, score breakdown, narrative, cross-references with
suppression reasons, actions.

Alert search by Alert ID navigates to the parent episode.

## 11.4 Actions

`Sign-off`, `Raise RFI`, `Add note`, `Move` — all executed by the supervisor
through the existing SCP workflow. The system executes none of them.

---

# 12. STAGE H — FEEDBACK

## 12.1 Why disposition alone is a bad label

Sign-off does not mean "not anomalous" — it may mean a false negative was never
investigated. Training on disposition teaches the system to reproduce existing
supervisor behaviour including its blind spots.

Capture three structured signals at review time:
1. **Grouping correct?** accept / should split / should merge with X
2. **Explanation useful?** yes / no
3. **Priority right?** too high / right / too low

Signal 1 is the highest-value label in the system because it supervises the
deterministic linking layer, which can be corrected precisely. Route grouping
corrections to **threshold review, not to a model.**

## 12.2 Control sample

Non-prioritised episodes are never reviewed, so they never generate labels, so
the system's view of them is never challenged. Left alone the loop confirms its
own priors indefinitely.

Route 2–5% of B3/B4 episodes for review each period, randomly selected. **This is
the only mechanism that produces a false-negative estimate.** Budget supervisor
capacity explicitly `[UNKNOWN #5]` — it will be the first thing cut under
pressure, and cutting it blinds the entire governance programme.

## 12.3 Governed change

- Rule weight changes: config change, replayed against history, approved,
  versioned
- IF retraining (if enabled): fixed cadence, champion/challenger shadow run,
  promotion criteria agreed in advance, model risk sign-off, new version with an
  effective-from date
- **Historical scores are never recomputed.**

---

# 13. CROSS-CUTTING

## 13.1 Configuration

- Every threshold, weight, tolerance, window and cap injected
- **No numeric literals in logic**
- Startup validation: fail loudly, refuse to start on missing config
- Config versioned; version stamped on every artifact it influenced
- Config changes are auditable events

## 13.2 Idempotency and replay

```
Stage keys:
  ingest      (alert_id, source_ruleset_version)
  signals     (episode_id, episode_version, signal_set_version)
  scoring     (episode_id, episode_version, scoring_version)
  narrative   (episode_id, episode_version, prompt_version, agent_model_version)
```

Replay produces identical output and commits nothing new.

**Build the replay harness as a deliverable, not deferred tooling.** It runs any
config version over any historical range, produces a diff report against the
incumbent, and persists the run as an evidence pack attached to the proposed
version. It is how thresholds get approved and how "why this value?" gets
answered in a governance forum.

## 13.3 Entitlements

Episode inherits its **most restrictive** member's entitlement. Grouping must
never become a mechanism to see an alert a supervisor is not entitled to.

Enforced in the data access layer, independently in the tool layer, never in a
prompt.

Episode spanning an entitlement boundary: redact non-entitled members, and
**record that the displayed evidence is partial** — the score was computed over
the full set and the supervisor sees a subset of its basis. Policy is
`[UNKNOWN #4]`; flag and stop.

## 13.4 Kill switch

One feature flag degrades the UI to a plain alert list. **Exercise it in
production readiness testing.** If it has never been pulled, you do not know it
works.

Separately: `scoring.isolation_forest.enabled = false` and an agent-layer
disable flag must each be independently exercisable.

## 13.5 Observability

```
Ingest    volume, DLQ rate by error class, blocklist hit rate per identifier
          per source system, identifier fill rate
Linking   tier mix over time, singleton rate, cap breaches, hub demotions
          (alarm on tier-mix AND singleton-rate shift together — that pair is
          the earliest warning that the CAL feed changed underneath you)
Signals   latency, missingness rate per signal, peer fallback rate
Scoring   latency, degraded rate, score distribution drift, clip rate
Queue     bucket distribution, B0 share, undated rate, starvation escalations
Agents    tool-call failure rate, validation rejection rate, budget-abort rate,
          latency p50/p95, tokens and cost per episode
Business  reconstruction time saved per review — the PRIMARY KPI. Baseline it
          BEFORE go-live or the project cannot be proven to have worked.
          Also: precision@k, % reviewed before deadline, grouping-correction rate
```

## 13.6 Latency budgets

```
CAL alert → episode in queue, p95            <= 90s
Signal computation per episode version, p95  <= 2s
Scoring per episode version, p95             <= 1s
Queue read (paginated), p95                  <= 500ms
Narrative generation on episode open, p95    <= 15s (async, with a loading state)
```

Narrative generation is user-facing and asynchronous. Render the deterministic
detail view immediately; stream or poll the narrative in.

---

# 14. TESTING

## 14.1 Property tests

These will not be caught by example-based tests and are the ones that matter.

```
Determinism         same inputs + versions → byte-identical signals, scores,
                    sort order. Shuffle input order N times.
Idempotence         reprocess → zero new rows
Point-in-time       no signal reads data with recorded_at > as_of_ts
                    (instrument the query layer; do not test by inspection)
Version guard       mismatched signal_set_version vs model artifact is rejected,
                    not scored
Sort totality       no two distinct episodes produce an equal sort key
Pagination          concatenating all pages yields every episode exactly once,
                    under concurrent writes
Agent groundedness  every numeric literal in a narrative appears in its trace
Spine independence  agent layer disabled → queue identical, ranking identical
IF independence     scoring.isolation_forest.enabled=false → ML module never
                    imported
```

## 14.2 Golden set

Five business archetypes with expected signals, scores, bands and explanations,
run on every release:

1. Cancel and rebook (CANCEL + LATE)
2. Cancel and correct (CANCEL + AMEND)
3. Late then corrected (LATE + AMEND)
4. Serial amendment (AMEND × n)
5. Standalone (single alert, episode of size 1 — **not a special case**)

## 14.3 Adversarial

NaN and inf signals, empty peer group, all-zeros identifiers, 500-member
episode, entitlement-split episode, signal-version skew, duplicate delivery,
clock skew, agent timeout mid-generation, injected instruction in free text,
missing deadline on every member, IF artifact hash mismatch at boot.

## 14.4 Agent-specific

- Injection corpus: free-text fixtures containing instruction-shaped content;
  assert no tool call outside the read-only set, assert validation catches
  injected claims
- Groundedness regression: fixed evidence packs, assert narratives contain no
  unsourced figures across N generations
- Budget: assert abort-to-template at the configured ceiling

---

# 15. BUILD ORDER

Do not parallelise across phases.

```
Phase 0   Ingest, normalise, [linking already built], signal store,
          rule-baseline score, deterministic queue, UI.
          Replay harness. Kill switch, exercised.
          NO ML. NO AGENTS.
          Shadow-run alongside the current process and MEASURE reconstruction
          time saved.

          This phase alone addresses the stated business problem and carries
          almost no model risk. Do not skip it to reach the agents faster.

Phase 1   Case builder and Analyst. Evidence pack, tool layer, narrative,
          output validation, trace persistence. Read-only throughout.
          Gated on UNKNOWN #7.

Phase 2   Review assistant and Drafting. Free-text ingestion with the full
          injection control set. Structured feedback capture. Control sample.

Phase 3   Isolation Forest behind the flag, with TreeSHAP. Admitted only on
          demonstrated lift over the rule baseline on a held-out labelled set,
          and only with model risk approval. Gated on UNKNOWN #6.
```

---

# 16. PROHIBITIONS

- Never write to SCP or the CAL alert store, by any path.
- Never give any agent a write tool.
- Never let an agent compute a number. Numbers come from tools.
- Never return raw row sets to an agent where a computed statistic will do.
- Never display an agent claim that does not trace to a tool result.
- Never rely on prompt instructions as an injection control.
- Never call `.fit()` in an inference path.
- Never refit a scaler at inference.
- Never use wall clock where `as_of_ts` is specified.
- Never count `episode_id` for history. Use `lineage_root_id`.
- Never re-derive a fact that linking already emitted in `structural_facts`.
- Never make the agent layer a dependency of the queue.
- Never render a degraded, partial or inferred result as a normal one.
- Never let missing data bury an episode.
- Never recompute a historical score or narrative with a newer version.
- Never invent a threshold. Read from config; fail loudly if absent.
- Never resolve ambiguity silently. Stop and ask.
